# Interfaces



# What is an Interface

An interface is a blueprint of a class.

It contains:

* abstract methods
* constants
* default/static methods (Java 8+)



##  Why Interfaces are Used

* Achieve abstraction
* Achieve multiple inheritance
* Define common behavior
* Loose coupling



# Syntax

interface Vehicle {
    void start();
}


# Implementing an Interface

class Car implements Vehicle {

    @Override
    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Car starts");
    }
}



# Important Rules

## Interface methods are:

* `public`
* `abstract`
  (by default)


## Interface variables are:

* `public`
* `static`
* `final`
  (by default)


# Example

interface Demo {

    int x = 10;

    void show();
}


Equivalent to:

public static final int x = 10;
public abstract void show();




# Multiple Inheritance using Interfaces

Java does not support multiple inheritance with classes.

But interfaces allow it.


## 📌 Example

interface A {
    void show();
}

interface B {
    void display();
}

class Test implements A, B {

    public void show() {}

    public void display() {}
}



# Default Methods (Java 8)

Interfaces can contain methods with body using `default`.


##  Example

interface Vehicle {

    default void stop() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle stopped");
    }
}



#  Static Methods in Interface

interface Demo {

    static void hello() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}


Usage:
Demo.hello();




# Functional Interface

An interface with only ONE abstract method.



## 📌 Example

@FunctionalInterface
interface Test {
    void show();
}


Used in:

* Lambda expressions
* Streams API

## Why Interfaces Don’t Have Constructors-

 * Constructors are used to initialize objects

 * But An interface cannot create objects

 * So Interfaces do not need constructors



#  Interface vs Abstract Class

| Feature              | Interface           | Abstract Class     |
| -------------------- | ------------------- | ------------------ |
| Methods              | Abstract by default | Abstract + Normal  |
| Variables            | Constants only      | Instance variables |
| Multiple Inheritance | ✅ Yes              | ❌ No               |
| Constructors         | ❌ No               | ✅ Yes              |
| Object creation	   | ❌ No	              |❌ Directly no

# 🔹 Best Practices

* Use interface for contracts
* Use abstract class for shared state/behavior
* Prefer interface for flexibility

